# Target C-II — host geometry forces every switching diameter a fixed angle away from the row axis

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` geometric strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** selected O--O path through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`; consequences stated for the deep exact-force branch  
**Dependencies:** coupled paired-remainder O--O path; exact diameter support-normal theorem; phase-free path-region switching identity; one-sided common-hub switching; exact-antipodal force-pair theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_host_forced_transverse_force_margin_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The previous deep-force argument used global C67 direction packing to delete force rays lying close to the exact reaction/row axis. The physical O--O host geometry gives a stronger pointwise statement, with no density loss.

Put

\[
c_*={4143\over10000}=0.4143,
\qquad
\vartheta_*:=\arccos c_*,
\qquad
\gamma_*:={\pi\over2}-\vartheta_*=\arcsin c_*.
\tag{HT.1}
\]

Numerically,

\[
\boxed{
\gamma_*=0.4271735560\ldots
=24.47524188\ldots^\circ.
}
\tag{HT.2}

Then every actual switching diameter `h` at every nonhub selected high-`t` interface satisfies

\[
\boxed{
 d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([d_h],[u_i])
 \ge\gamma_*-o(1),
}
\tag{HT.3}

or equivalently

\[
\boxed{
|d_h\cdot Ju_i|\ge0.4143-o(1).
}
\tag{HT.4}

This applies to every switching diameter, not merely to a positive-density extraction.

---

## 1. The two physical host arcs have width strictly below a semicircle

The coupled path uses terminal-energy threshold

\[
e_\tau\le0.5857+o(1).
\]

At either terminal of a selected O--O train, if `alpha` is the deviation of the outward support normal from the corresponding ideal oriented normal branch, the terminal-energy estimate gives

\[
1-\cos\alpha\le e_\tau+o(1).
\]

Hence

\[
\cos\alpha\ge0.4143-o(1),
\qquad
|\alpha|\le\vartheta_*+o(1).
\tag{HT.5}

All selected row phases lie in one shrinking projective window about `[u_*]`. The two ideal terminal-normal branches are therefore `+Ju_*` and `-Ju_*` up to `o(1)`. Outward support-normal angle is monotone along a convex boundary, so every support normal on either entire physical host arc lies within

\[
\vartheta_*+o(1)
\]

of the corresponding branch.

Thus each host arc has support-normal width at most

\[
2\vartheta_*+o(1)<\pi.
\tag{HT.6}

---

## 2. A diameter direction is an exact support normal at its endpoint

Let `xy` be an actual diameter edge, `|x-y|=D`. For every `z` in the source hull,

\[
|z-y|^2\le D^2=|x-y|^2.
\]

Expanding around `x` gives

\[
2(x-y)\cdot(z-x)+|z-x|^2\le0,
\]

so

\[
(x-y)\cdot(z-x)\le0
\quad\text{for every }z.
\]

Therefore `x-y` is an exact outward support normal at `x`; similarly `y-x` is an exact outward support normal at `y`.

Now take a switching diameter at nonhub interface `i`. The path-region switching identity and one-sided hub routing give one unique birth endpoint in the physical layer `R_i`. Every diameter endpoint is extreme, so that birth endpoint lies on one of the two layer host subarcs. The diameter line is therefore one of the support-normal lines covered by (HT.5).

Since `[u_i]=[u_*]+o(1)`, the projective triangle inequality gives

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([d_h],[u_i])
\ge {\pi\over2}-\vartheta_*-o(1)
=\gamma_*-o(1),
\]

proving (HT.3)--(HT.4).

The near-disk radial/midpoint approximation is not needed.

---

## 3. Exact force pairs get a fixed two-sided dipole cusp

At an exact-antipodal interface the reaction axis equals the row line projectively:

\[
e_i={G_i\over|G_i|}=\pm u_i.
\]

Choose the exact positive force pair `h_i^-,h_i^+`. Both are switching diameter edges, so (HT.4) applies to both.

For the elementary exact-antipodal dipole coordinate `d^(i)`, the squared-length derivative on an actual diameter bar is

\[
\ell_h(s d^{(i)})
=2Ds\,d_h\cdot Je_i
\]

up to the common interface orientation sign. Hence

\[
\boxed{
|\ell_{h_i^\pm}(s d^{(i)})|
\ge(0.8286-o(1))D|s|.
}
\tag{HT.7}

The two force bars have opposite transverse signs, so one lengthens for either sign of `s`.

If their lifted deviations from `e_i` are `p_i,q_i>0`, `p_i+q_i<pi`, the exact force coefficients are

\[
\lambda_i^-={|G_i|\over D}{\sin q_i\over\sin(p_i+q_i)},
\qquad
\lambda_i^+={|G_i|\over D}{\sin p_i\over\sin(p_i+q_i)}.
\]

The projective margin gives

\[
\sin p_i,\sin q_i\ge0.4143-o(1),
\]

while `sin(p_i+q_i)<=1`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\lambda_i^\pm
\ge(0.4143-o(1)){|G_i|\over D}.
}
\tag{HT.8}

No absolute multiplier floor is claimed because `|G_i|/D` may vanish.

---

## 4. The old axis-near density deletion disappears

In the parameterized deep-force construction, the previous proof deleted up to

\[
2\delta D+o(D)
\]

centers solely to ensure that both selected force bars stayed away from the row axis. Sections 1--3 show that every selected switching bar already has the stronger fixed margin `gamma_*-o(1)`. That deletion is unnecessary.

Combined with the host-sharpened robust fork, the no-positive-density-strong-TB1 branch starts with

\[
|E_D|>c_0D-o(D),
\qquad
c_0={113\over20{,}000{,}000}.
\]

After imposing pair gap at least `rho` and deleting radius-`r` separator neighborhoods of density `eta`, the deep exact-force family has

\[
\boxed{
(c_0-2\rho-\eta)D-o(D)
}
\tag{HT.9}

centers, each with the fixed host margin (HT.3), exact positive force compression, bilateral birth, the common inverse-`D` train grid, and arbitrary prescribed finite exact-phase depth.

With the displayed choices

\[
\rho={c_0\over100},
\qquad
\eta={c_0\over50},
\]

this becomes

\[
\boxed{0.96c_0=5.424\times10^{-6}.}
\tag{HT.10}

The existing diagonal argument permits exact-phase depth tending to infinity while preserving this leading density. The normalized KKT scale may vanish, but the geometric dipole slope remains at least `0.8286-o(1)` after division by `D`.

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The exact-force branch can now be normalized as

```text
(c0-o(1))D deep exact-force centers
+ arbitrary prescribed/growing exact-phase depth
+ bilateral ordered birth
+ both force bars at least 24.475 degrees from the row/reaction line
+ dipole slope at least (0.8286-o(1))D on each selected bar
+ force coefficients >=(0.4143-o(1))|G_i|/D
+ one common inverse-D near-triangular train grid.
```

The `axis-near force ray` degeneration is gone pointwise, rather than merely at positive density. C-II remains open because the exact KKT scale can still vanish and the fixed-transverse force pairs remain compatible with the current global endpoint order.

**Disposition:** every switching diameter on the selected high-`t` path inherits a fixed source-geometric transverse margin directly from exact diameter support-normality; the deep exact-force branch keeps this margin with no C67 axis-near deletion.